In fact, a module map $f : R^ n \to M$ corresponds to a choice of elements $x_1, x_2, \ldots , x_ n$ of $M$ (namely, the images of the standard basis elements $e_1, e_2, \ldots , e_ n$); furthermore, an element $x \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(f)$ corresponds to a relation between these $x_1, x_2, \ldots , x_ n$ (namely, the relation $\sum _ i f_ i x_ i = 0$, where the $f_ i$ are the coordinates of $x$). The module map $h$ (represented as an $m \times n$-matrix) corresponds to the matrix $(a_{ij})$ from Lemma <a href="/tag/00HK" data-tag="00HK">10.39.11</a>, and the $y_ j$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/00HK" data-tag="00HK">10.39.11</a> are the images of the standard basis vectors of $R^ m$ under $g$.